<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sistine Madonna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1512–<a href="/years/1513">1513</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sistine Madonna is an oil painting by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, made about 1512–1513 for the church of San Sisto in Piacenza. It shows the Virgin and Child on clouds between Pope Sixtus II and Saint Barbara, with two winged putti leaning on a ledge below. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> bought it in 1754 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, where it hangs in the Old Masters Picture Gallery; taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1945, it was returned in 1955.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sistine-madonna.jpg" alt="Sistine Madonna" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RAFAEL_-_Madonna_Sixtina_(Gem%C3%A4ldegalerie_Alter_Meister,_Dresden,_1513-14._%C3%93leo_sobre_lienzo,_265_x_196_cm).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sistine Madonna, also called the Madonna di San Sisto, was commissioned in 1512 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> in honour of his late uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, as an altarpiece for the church of the Benedictine monastery of San Sisto in Piacenza, with which the Rovere family had a long-standing relationship. The commission required that the painting show both Saint Sixtus and Saint Barbara. It was one of the last Madonnas painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>. Giorgio Vasari called it a truly rare and extraordinary work. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Antonio da Correggio</a>, on first seeing it, cried out that he too was a painter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The oil painting on canvas measures 265 by 196 centimetres. The Madonna, holding the Christ Child and flanked by Saint Sixtus and Saint Barbara, stands on clouds before dozens of obscured putti. Two distinctive winged putti rest on their elbows beneath her. Pigment analysis shows the usual pigments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, among them malachite mixed with orpiment in the green drapery, natural ultramarine mixed with lead white in the Madonna's blue robe, and lead-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> yellow, vermilion and lead white in Saint Barbara's yellow sleeve.</p><p>The putti are well known in their own right and have been printed on stamps, postcards and wrapping paper. Stories grew around them: one tells that the children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>'s model came to watch him paint and that he added them as he saw them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1754 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> bought the painting for 110,000 to 120,000 francs, the highest price paid for any painting for many decades, and it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>. Johann Joachim Winckelmann praised it in his history of ancient art of 1764, and a legend of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>'s Dream&quot; arose in the late 18th century. It became an icon of German Romanticism and influenced Goethe, Wagner and Nietzsche. Dostoyevsky called it a revelation of the human spirit. In 1855 it was given a room of its own in the new museum designed by Gottfried Semper.</p><p>It survived the bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in the war, stored in a tunnel in Saxon Switzerland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where it was shown at the Pushkin Museum in 1946. In 1955, after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, the Soviets returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Western reports that it had been damaged in storage were denied by Soviet witnesses. It hangs in the Gemäldegalerie Alte Meister in Dresden, which marked its 500th anniversary in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sistine Madonna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Augustus the Strong and Augustus III Dresden became one of the finest Baroque cities in Europe, with the Zwinger, the Frauenkirche, the Green Vault treasury and a picture gallery that acquired Raphael’s <b>Sistine Madonna</b> in 1754.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
